<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Macrocytic anemia with mild leukopenia and thrombocytopenia in a woman of 64, with very low B12, raised MMA, and positive intrinsic factor antibodies. Please confirm pernicious anemia and advise on replacement, monitoring, and gastric risk. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks; B12 injections start tomorrow. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do these results establish pernicious anemia, and do the low white cell and platelet counts or the raised LDH need a bone marrow examination or a hemolysis work-up?</item><item>Which replacement schedule do you advise with these neurologic findings, and is oral B12 an option for her?</item><item>Which tests should confirm the response, and when? Should the B12 level be repeated?</item><item>What gastric evaluation is needed for gastric cancer and neuroendocrine tumor risk, and how often?</item><item>What else should we watch for, such as iron deficiency, potassium, or thyroid disease?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Oakley is seen at the request of Dr. Ellingsen one week after starting intramuscular cyanocobalamin for macrocytic anemia with low B12 and positive intrinsic factor antibodies. She has had all seven daily injections. Her tongue is less sore and her energy slightly better; the foot tingling and unsteadiness in the dark are unchanged. She has no bleeding, bruising, fevers, weight loss, or night sweats. No alcohol excess, nitrous oxide exposure, metformin, or acid suppressants. She teaches shooting classes and wants to keep doing so. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Annie Oakley was born Phoebe Ann Moses (also spelled Mosey) on August 13, 1860, in Darke County, Ohio, to Jacob Moses and Susan Wise Moses. [D]</item><item>Her father died when she was six; two sources give pneumonia as the cause, and the Garst Museum says he died from exposure to winter weather. Her mother was left to support a large family. [D]</item><item>She became a celebrated exhibition sharpshooter who toured with a Wild West show in the United States and Europe, and in later years she gave exhibitions and taught women to shoot. [D]</item><item>On October 29, 1901, the show&apos;s train collided head-on with another train in North Carolina; Oakley&apos;s back was injured, and the accident ended her touring with the show. [D]</item><item>Late in 1922 she was severely injured in an automobile accident in Florida, reported as fractures of the hip and ankle, after which she had to wear a brace on her leg; her recovery took more than a year. [D]</item><item>Her health declined in the years after the accident, and in 1925 she moved back to Darke County, Ohio. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

