<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Pernicious anemia with B12 deficiency neuropathy (D51.0). Established. Very low B12 with raised MMA confirms deficiency, and intrinsic factor antibodies are highly specific for pernicious anemia. The mild leukopenia and thrombocytopenia, raised LDH, and raised bilirubin come from ineffective red cell production in the marrow. The reticulocyte rise to 6.8 percent at one week, with counts already climbing, confirms the diagnosis. A bone marrow examination is not needed unless the counts fail to normalize by eight weeks. The neurologic signs show dorsal column involvement, so treatment must be by injection and must not lapse. [S]</item><item>Autoimmune gastritis and gastric risk. Pernicious anemia is the late stage of autoimmune gastritis, which raises the risk of type 1 gastric neuroendocrine tumors and gastric adenocarcinoma. She has never had an endoscopy. [S]</item><item>Associated conditions. Autoimmune thyroid disease is common with autoimmune gastritis; her TSH is normal. Iron deficiency often appears as red cell production recovers, because absorption of dietary iron is reduced without gastric acid. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: The presenting blood count, reticulocytes, B12, folate, MMA, intrinsic factor antibody, LDH, bilirubin, iron studies, TSH, and kidney function; today&apos;s blood count, reticulocyte count of 6.8 percent, and potassium of 3.7 mmol/L. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Continue cyanocobalamin 1000 micrograms intramuscularly: weekly for four weeks after the daily week, then monthly for life. Oral B12 is not advised for her while neurologic signs are present, and injections should never be stopped. [S]</item><item>If neurologic signs stop improving or symptoms return before each monthly dose, shorten the interval. [S]</item><item>Blood count with MCV and MMA at eight weeks; do not repeat the B12 level. [S]</item><item>Ferritin and transferrin saturation at eight weeks; if iron deficiency appears, treat it and complete the GI evaluation. [S]</item><item>Upper endoscopy with biopsies of the gastric body and antrum in separate jars, of any polyp or nodule, and of the duodenum, with H. pylori testing. Surveillance after that by the findings: about every three years for advanced atrophy, every one to two years if neuroendocrine tumors are found. [S]</item><item>TSH once a year. [S]</item><item>Avoid nitrous oxide anesthesia, which inactivates B12. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Ellingsen today; the patient agrees and has the plan in writing. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Hematology will see her again if the blood counts do not normalize by eight weeks or if new cytopenias appear. Primary care continues the injections and monitoring on the CMID schedule. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>
